Changes in v6:
- commit message: restore the motivation the mailed v5 body opened with
  (replenish is the last open-path hypercall still assuming queue 0) and
  the netconsole reason a printk must not run under replenish_lock;
  argue the correlator change once, in the paragraph that makes it,
  rather than also opening with its consequence; and move the remaining
  behaviour deltas below the restructuring narrative instead of stacking
  all four ahead of it
- initialise the replenish locks once in probe; open() no longer
  reinitialises them in alloc_rx_queues(), where it could reset a lock
  another CPU was holding through poll_controller()
- pass DMA_ATTR_NO_WARN to dma_map_single_attrs() in the replenish
  loop. The iommu warning could re-enter via netconsole and deadlock
  on the replenish_lock this CPU already holds
- static_assert IBMVETH_MAX_RX_REGULAR == 8 and
  IBMVETH_MAX_RX_PER_HCALL == 12 against the hand-written
  legacy descs[0..7] and MQ ioba[0..5] argument lists
- add clarifying comment on legacy 8-buffer hcall batch bound
- drop the three buffer-submit hypercall counters along with the rest
  of hcall_stats; see patch 7
- fix the KUnit fixtures to allocate a dummy free_map
- align dma_map_single_attrs() arguments; restore newline at EOF
- commit message: give the real reason for replenish_lock. It claimed
  the lock serialises concurrent per-queue replenish walks, but pools
  are per queue and so is the lock, so those walks never contend. The
  contention is producer against consumer on one queue, which netpoll
  can drive from outside NAPI

